<h1>Learn</h1>
<p class="sub">interactive tutorials — every figure below is computed live by the running system</p>

<p>Each panel teaches one part of Orpheus by letting you drive it. The widgets are Facet
<code>Live</code> holes: the server renders the first value, and edits re-evaluate just that
result. Nothing here is a recording — drag a slider and the algorithm genuinely runs, on
<b>Loom</b>, from the axioms up.</p>

<!-- ===================== Algorithms, played ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">Algorithms, played — <code>import algoviz</code></div>
  <div class="b">
    <p class="lede">The <code>algoviz</code> library instruments classic algorithms: they run
    normally, but every elementary step — a comparison, a swap, a probe, a visit — appends a
    <em>frame</em> to a trace. A trace is ordinary data (a 0-terminated list), and a frame renders
    to a <code>gfx</code> scene, so the same SVG pipeline that draws <code>latte gfx</code> draws
    each step. Drag <code>k</code> to step; edit <code>xs</code> and the trace recomputes.</p>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>Bubble sort.</b> Adjacent bars are compared (yellow) and swapped when out
      of order (red); after each pass the largest unsorted value has bubbled into its final place
      (green). Watch the green region grow from the right.</div>
      {{ Live.view("AlgoViz.frame(\"bubble\", xs, k, 0)", [["xs", "5 2 8 1 9 3"], ["k", "0", "~", "0", "40", "1"]]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>Insertion sort.</b> Each element walks left through the already-sorted
      prefix until it fits — the way a hand of cards is sorted. Compare how its step count differs
      from bubble sort on the same input: <code>AlgoViz.steps</code> is the trace length.</div>
      {{ Live.view("AlgoViz.frame(\"insert\", xs, k, 0)", [["xs", "5 2 8 1 9 3"], ["k", "0", "~", "0", "40", "1"]]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>Binary search.</b> The list is sorted first; each probe (yellow) halves
      the live window (blue) until the target is found (green) or the window closes. Change
      <code>t</code> and watch the probes route differently — at most ⌈log₂ n⌉ + 1 of them.</div>
      {{ Live.view("AlgoViz.frame(\"binsearch\", xs, k, t)", [["xs", "5 2 8 1 9 3"], ["k", "0", "~", "0", "8", "1"], ["t", "8", "~", "0", "12", "1"]]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>Breadth-first search.</b> A wave (yellow frontier, blue visited) floods a
      maze one ring per step; when it reaches the goal (red), parents are walked back and the
      shortest path is painted green. BFS visits in distance order — that is <em>why</em> the first
      arrival is the shortest route.</div>
      {{ Live.view("AlgoViz.frame(\"bfs\", \"\", k, 0)", [["k", "0", "~", "0", "16", "1"]]) }}
    </div>

    <p class="lede">Step counts for the current input:
    bubble {{ AlgoViz.steps("bubble", "5 2 8 1 9 3", 0) }},
    insertion {{ AlgoViz.steps("insert", "5 2 8 1 9 3", 0) }},
    binary search {{ AlgoViz.steps("binsearch", "5 2 8 1 9 3", 8) }},
    BFS {{ AlgoViz.steps("bfs", "", 0) }}.
    The traces are plain Latte values — open <a href="/api/source?name=algoviz">the library's
    source</a> to see how little instrumentation costs.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== The Latte language ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">The Latte language — a four-step tutorial</div>
  <div class="b">
    <p class="lede">Latte is the system's functional language, bootstrapped from one primitive
    (successor). Four ideas carry most of it; each box below is live — edit and see.</p>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>1 · Everything is a Knot.</b> An atom is a natural number; a cell is a
      pair. Lists are right-nested cells ending in <code>0</code> — so always write the trailing
      zero: <code>[1 2 3 0]</code> is a list, <code>[1 2 3]</code> is nested pairs.</div>
      {{ Live.box("Latte.eval(expr)", ["expr", "(len [1 2 3 0])"]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>2 · 0 means true.</b> Comparisons return the <em>loobean</em> 0 for yes —
      try <code>(lt 2 3)</code>, <code>(5 == 6)</code>, or an <code>if</code> around either.</div>
      {{ Live.box("Latte.eval(expr)", ["expr", "if (lt 2 3) then 100 else 200"]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>3 · Gates are values.</b> <code>fn [x] -&gt; body</code> is a closure you
      can pass to <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>foldl</code>.</div>
      {{ Live.box("Latte.eval(expr)", ["expr", "(map (fn [x] -> (mul x x)) [1 2 3 4 0])"]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>4 · Iterate with loop/again.</b> The one looping construct: named
      accumulators, re-entered in tail position. Here, Fibonacci.</div>
      {{ Live.box("Latte.eval(expr)", ["expr", "loop with [a = 0, b = 1, i = 10] : if (i == 0) then a else again(b, (add a b), (dec i)) end"]) }}
    </div>

    <p class="lede">To keep a definition for your whole session, use the System console
    (<code>latte gui</code> → System): <code>def sq [x] = (mul x x)</code>, then
    <code>eval (sq 7)</code> — from any text, the Oberon way.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== The tools, as tutorials ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">The system's tools — one live taste of each</div>
  <div class="b">
    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>SCArs</b> — the sound-change engine. A Solar Speech word evolves through
      the full rule cascade into Heart Speech. Try <code>serdā</code>, <code>nūn</code>,
      <code>kósmā</code>.</div>
      {{ Live.box("SCArs.evolve(word)", ["word", "serdā"]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>Sentiment</b> — one engine, two market-correct views. The general score
      reads financial positivity; the bond score reads the hawk/dove policy axis and treats
      risk-off as a Treasury bid. The same text can be bullish for stocks and bearish for bonds —
      try "hot inflation forces aggressive hikes".</div>
      {{ Live.box("Sent.label(text)", ["text", "markets rally on dovish pivot"]) }}
      {{ Live.box("Sent.bond(text)", ["text", "markets rally on dovish pivot"]) }}
    </div>

    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap"><b>The market lab</b> — the built-in BTC/USD series, charted live; the same
      data the trading advisor trains on. Slide <code>n</code> for the window.</div>
      {{ Live.view("Viz.chart(kind, Mkt.series(n))", [["kind", "line", "line", "bars"], ["n", "120", "~", "10", "1318", "1"]]) }}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== Latte from the GUI ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">Latte from the GUI — the full workflow</div>
  <div class="b">
    <p class="lede">The boxes above are one corner of it. In the GUI proper (the <b>System</b>
    viewer), any command line in any text runs on middle-click; <code>def</code> turns a
    one-liner into a session function; <code>System.New</code> opens a module frame whose
    <b>Compile</b> loads your code into the running image and whose <b>Store</b> persists it as
    a package; and an arm returning <code>[%html …]</code> or <code>[%svg …]</code> embeds as a
    live object where the command ran. The complete walkthrough — from a first
    <code>eval</code> to a compiled, persistent, rendering tool of your own — is
    <a href="/docs#using-latte-from-the-gui"><b>Using Latte from the GUI</b></a> on the
    documentation shelf. And the language tutorial itself is executable: open
    <a href="/docs#latte-tutorial"><b>The Latte Tutorial</b></a> in the GUI's Docs viewer
    and middle-click its examples — the text you are learning from runs.</p>
    <div class="try">
      <div class="cap">The same evaluator the console uses, as a page widget — try a
      <code>def</code>'d name here after defining it in the System console, and it resolves:
      one scope, everywhere.</div>
      {{ Live.box("Latte.eval(expr)", ["expr", "(sum (range 101))"]) }}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Deeper: <a href="/tools">every tool as a live widget</a> · <a href="/docs">the documentation
